###### Abstract

The repeating fast radio burst (FRB) localized to a globular cluster in M81 challenges our understanding of FRB models. In this _Letter_, we explore dynamical formation scenarios for objects in old globular clusters that may plausibly power FRBs. Using N-body simulations, we demonstrate that young neutron stars may form in globular clusters at a rate of up to \\sim 50\\,\\mathrm{Gpc^{-3}\\,yr^{-1}} through a combination of binary white dwarf mergers, white dwarf-neutron star mergers, binary neutron star mergers, and accretion induced collapse of massive white dwarfs in binary systems. We consider two FRB emission mechanisms: First, we show that a magnetically-powered source (e.g., a magnetar with field strength \\gtrsim 10^{14}\\,\\mathrm{G}) is viable for radio emission efficiencies \\gtrsim 10^{-4}. This would require magnetic activity lifetimes longer than the associated spin-down timescales and longer than empirically-constrained lifetimes of Galactic magnetars. Alternatively, if these dynamical formation channels produce young rotation-powered neutron stars with spin periods of \\sim 10\\,\\mathrm{ms} and magnetic fields of \\sim 10^{11}\\,\\mathrm{G} (corresponding to spin-down lifetimes of \\gtrsim 10^{5}\\,\\mathrm{yr}), the inferred event rate and energetics can be reasonably reproduced for order unity duty cycles. Additionally, we show that recycled millisecond pulsars or low-mass X-ray binaries similar to those well-observed in Galactic globular clusters may also be plausible channels, but only if their duty cycle for producing bursts similar to the M81 FRB is small.

