In the magnetically-powered scenario, the intrinsic energy budget is estimated as E_{\\rm mag}\\approx(B_{\\rm NS}^{2}B_{\\rm NS}^{3})/6. As in Beloborodov and Li (2016); Margalit et al. (2019), we estimate the magnetic activity timescale in the modified Urca case as

\\tau_{\\rm mag}\\approx 2\\times 10^{5}\\Bigg{(}\\frac{B_{\\rm NS}}{10^{14}\\,{\\rm G }}\\Bigg{)}^{-1.2}\\Bigg{(}\\frac{L}{10^{5}\\,{\\rm cm}}\\Bigg{)}^{1.6}\\,{\\rm yr},  assuming \\delta B\\sim B/2 as the amplitude of magnetic field fluctuations over an (uncertain) length-scale L within the NS core. In this case, the characteristic luminosity from magnetic activity is

\\langle\\dot{E}_{\\rm mag}\\rangle\\approx E_{\\rm mag}/\\tau_{\\rm mag}\\approx 3 \\times 10^{32}\\Bigg{(}\\frac{B_{\\rm NS}}{10^{14}\\,{\\rm G}}\\Bigg{)}^{3.2}\\,{\\rm erg }\\,{\\rm s}^{-1}. 

By combining Equations (2) and (3) with the inferred time-averaged luminosity, we find radio emission efficiencies of f_{\\rm r}\\gtrsim 10^{-4} (and field strengths of roughly 2\\times 10^{14}\\,{\\rm G}) are required to produce active lifetimes, \\tau_{\\rm mag}\\approx 10^{5}\\,{\\rm yr}, necessary to explain the FRB event rate for the young NS formation scenarios discussed in Section 3. Although highly uncertain, radio emission efficiencies this high have been demonstrated by some FRB models (e.g., Plotnikov and Sironi, 2019; Lu et al., 2020).

In the rotation-power scenario, the intrinsic energy budget is simply the rotational energy of the NS: E_{\\rm rot}\\approx 2\\pi^{2}IP_{\\rm NS}^{-2} where I\\approx(2/5)MR^{2} is the moment of inertia of the NS and P_{\\rm NS} is the NS spin period. The characteristic spin-down timescale of a magnetic dipole is

\\tau_{\\rm sd}\\approx\\frac{P}{2\\dot{P}}\\approx 5\\times 10^{5}\\,{\\rm yr}\\Bigg{(} \\frac{P_{\\rm NS}}{10\\,{\\rm ms}}\\Bigg{)}^{2}\\Bigg{(}\\frac{B_{\\rm NS}}{10^{11} \\,{\\rm G}}\\Bigg{)}^{-2},  which yields a characteristic spin-down luminosity of

\\dot{E}_{\\rm rot}\\approx 10^{37}\\Bigg{(}\\frac{B_{\\rm NS}}{10^{11}\\,{\\rm G}} \\Bigg{)}^{2}\\Bigg{(}\\frac{P_{\\rm NS}}{10\\,{\\rm ms}}\\Bigg{)}^{-4}\\,{\\rm erg}\\,{ \\rm s}^{-1}. 

For f_{\\rm r}\\gtrsim 10^{-8}, the spin-down luminosity is sufficient to supply the energy required to power the FRB for a \\tau_{\\rm sd}\\gtrsim 10^{5}\\,{\\rm yr} lifetime.

